The sums \(S_r\) are defined as sums of binomial coefficients modulo 502. Using properties of finite fields and roots of unity modulo the prime 503, we reduce \(S_r\) modulo 503 to:

\[
S_r \equiv \begin{cases}
\binom{462}{r} \mod 503 & \text{if } 0 \leq r \leq 462, \\
0 \mod 503 & \text{if } 463 \leq r \leq 501.
\end{cases}
\]

Since \(\binom{462}{r}\) is not divisible by 503 for \(0 \leq r \leq 462\) (because 503 > 462), the only values of \(S_r\) divisible by 503 are those with \(463 \leq r \leq 501\). There are \(501 - 463 + 1 = 39\) such integers.

\boxed{39}

We have prime \(p = 503\) and \(N = 10000 = ap + b\) with \(a = 19\), \(b = 443\), so \(a + b = 462\). For each \(r\) with \(0 \le r < 502\), \(S_r = \sum_{k \equiv r \pmod{502}} \binom{10000}{k}\). Using properties of binomial coefficients modulo a prime, we show that \(S_r \equiv \binom{462}{r} \pmod{503}\).

Since \(\binom{462}{r}\) is not divisible by \(503\) for \(0 \le r \le 462\) (as \(503 > 462\)), and \(\binom{462}{r} = 0\) for \(r > 462\), we conclude that \(S_r\) is a multiple of \(503\) exactly when \(463 \le r \le 501\). The number of such \(r\) is \(501 - 463 + 1 = 39\).

\boxed{39}

The sums \(S_r = \sum_{m \geq 0} \binom{10000}{502m + r}\) are considered modulo the prime \(503\). Using properties of binomial coefficients in characteristic \(p\) and reduction modulo \(x^{502} - 1\), it is shown that in \(\mathbb{F}_{503}[x]\), the generating polynomial \(S(x) = \sum_{r=0}^{501} S_r x^r\) satisfies \(S(x) = (1+x)^{462}\). Therefore, \(S_r \equiv \binom{462}{r} \pmod{503}\) for \(0 \le r \le 501\).

Since \(503 > 462\), the binomial coefficients \(\binom{462}{r}\) are not divisible by \(503\) for \(0 \le r \le 462\), but \(\binom{462}{r} = 0\) for \(r > 462\). Thus, \(S_r\) is a multiple of \(503\) if and only if \(r > 462\). The number of such \(r\) in \(\{0, 1, \dots, 501\}\) is \(501 - 463 + 1 = 39\).

